<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Barony of Karytaina</h1><p class="st-meta">1209 CE – 1320 CE (approx.) · capital Karytaina</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Frankish barony in the mountains of central Peloponnese, guarded by the castle of Karytaina and celebrated through its lord Geoffrey of Bruyeres in the Chronicle of the Morea.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The barony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karytaina">Karytaina</a>, also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skorta">Skorta</a> after its mountainous district, was created around 1209 in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea</a> and granted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-of-bruyeres">Hugh of Bruyeres</a>. With 22 knight's fiefs it was one of the largest baronies, set to control the unruly highlands of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alpheios">Alpheios</a> valley.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Hugh's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-of-bruyeres">Geoffrey of Bruyeres</a> built the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karytaina">Karytaina</a> on a crag above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alpheios">Alpheios</a> around 1254. He was the model knight of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-the-morea">Chronicle of the Morea</a>, admired for his courage despite rebelling against Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II</a> and eloping with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>'s wife. Greek villagers and local archons held land under the barons.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Geoffrey died in 1275 without a male heir, half the barony reverted to the prince and its fortunes declined. In 1320 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-asen">Andronikos Asen</a>, the Byzantine governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mistras</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karytaina">Karytaina</a> and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skorta">Skorta</a>, ending Frankish rule there.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/barony-of-karytaina.jpg" alt="Barony of Karytaina" width="320" height="251"><figcaption>Image: Peloponnese_Middle_Ages_map-fr.svg: Eric Gaba (Sting - fr:Sting) derivative work: Constantine ✍ · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peloponnese_Middle_Ages_map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1209 CE – 1320 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Karytaina</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hugh of Bruyeres, Geoffrey of Bruyeres</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Eastern Orthodox Christianity (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French, Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Greece</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Barony of Skorta</dd></dl></aside></div>

